    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d31">

                <pb facs="0140" n="36" sameAs="frus:frus1933v02/pg36"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">702.0641/84</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain (Bingham) to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 182</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1933-09-07">September 7,
                        1933</date>.<lb/>[Received September 21.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to the
                    Department’s instruction No. 58 of August 15, 1933, directing me to take up
                    informally with the British authorities the question of the taxes on automobiles
                    operated by American consular officers stationed in Great Britain and Northern
                    Ireland, with a view to ascertaining whether exemption is granted to any foreign
                    consular officers stationed in Great Britain and whether exemption would be
                    granted to American consular officers from taxes on their automobiles, on a
                    reciprocal basis.</p>
                <p>This matter was taken up informally with the appropriate Foreign Office official
                    on August 29, and I enclose a copy of a letter dated September 6<anchor n="37"
                        xml:id="d31-f1"/> which the Embassy has received in reply. This letter
                    explains that no exemptions are granted to foreign consular officers in the
                    United Kingdom with respect to the payment of annual taxes on automobiles, and
                    also gives the Foreign Office’s view that it would not be possible to make any
                    arrangements for the exemption of United States Consuls from payment of these
                    taxes, on a reciprocal basis.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>For the Ambassador: <lb/>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ray Atherton</hi>
                    </signed>
                    <lb/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Counselor of Embassy</hi>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
